Anything, Mark.

00:07:40.940 --> 00:07:45.260
I could track anything,
mail merge, send emails

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:49.070
when things are overdue,
a lot of different things

00:07:49.070 --> 00:07:51.410
you can do from a Sheet even
though it's not necessarily

00:07:51.410 --> 00:07:52.640
built to be--

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:55.430
I think Sheets is one
of those products that

00:07:55.430 --> 00:07:58.910
was built to do one thing and
now it does thousands of things

00:07:58.910 --> 00:08:00.680
because it is so flexible.

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:01.996
So that's my favorite product.

00:08:01.996 --> 00:08:04.370
JOANNA SMITH: I've got to say,
I'm voting for Gmail here.

00:08:04.370 --> 00:08:06.980
Not even Inbox, just old
school classic Gmail.

00:08:06.980 --> 00:08:09.330
Because one, I don't
know if you remember,

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:13.280
but way back when we all signed
up for Gmail a long time ago,

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:16.280
it was the first email
that was ever threaded.

00:08:16.280 --> 00:08:19.220
So your reply to an email
showed up next to the email

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:20.720
that you originally got.

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:22.580
And I remember that
changing the way

00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:25.470
that I viewed speaking to
people on the internet, I guess.

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:27.680
But the other thing is
that Gmail has grown,

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:31.340
and it's this powerhouse
of great features

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:32.990
to help you keep
your life in order.

00:08:32.990 --> 00:08:35.159
And the way that I
can prove that is,

00:08:35.159 --> 00:08:37.700
we all can't deny that
Google runs on email.

00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:41.500
Everything from our bug systems,
our CLs, our code submissions,

00:08:41.500 --> 00:08:44.900
is the build broken, this is
all handled through email.

00:08:44.900 --> 00:08:47.990
And that's why we had
to create our own email

00:08:47.990 --> 00:08:50.250
service, because no
others could keep up.

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:52.430
And that's why it's been
such a successful email

00:08:52.430 --> 00:08:53.820
provider for so many people.

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:55.410
And also for so many companies.

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:57.650
So it's my favorite because
it's an oldie but goodie.

00:08:57.650 --> 00:08:58.900
MARK MANDEL: That sounds good.

00:08:58.900 --> 00:09:01.940
So Alicia, you were talking
about how you could do anything

00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:02.780
in Sheets.

00:09:02.780 --> 00:09:04.550
How did you do
anything in Sheets?

00:09:04.550 --> 00:09:06.883
Like, you were talking about
sending emails from Sheets.

00:09:06.883 --> 00:09:09.050
Like how-- there's no
spreadsheet function

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:11.360
for sending email
in a cell, is there?

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:12.319
Or, how does that work?

00:09:12.319 --> 00:09:14.609
ALICIA WILLIAMS: Well, that's
more of a recent addition

00:09:14.609 --> 00:09:16.310
to the types of
things that I can do,

00:09:16.310 --> 00:09:18.200
but that's through Apps Script.

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:21.230
So Apps Script is a JavaScript
based language, I've

00:09:21.230 --> 00:09:21.860
been learning.

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:24.770
So I spent many years
just using the UI,

00:09:24.770 --> 00:09:27.140
doing everything you can
do in Sheets, and Docs,

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:28.700
and Gmail with the UI.

00:09:28.700 --> 00:09:30.950
And then I started
discovering that behind

00:09:30.950 --> 00:09:32.960
a lot of the spreadsheets
and docs at work

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:35.480
were these scripts that
other people had written.

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:39.390
I started dabbling in those,
editing them when I needed to,

00:09:39.390 --> 00:09:40.910
and then finally
decided it was time

00:09:40.910 --> 00:09:43.670
to learn to write my own to
do things that no one else had

00:09:43.670 --> 00:09:44.840
written to do.

00:09:44.840 --> 00:09:47.600
Yeah, it's something that you
can write standalone scripts

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:50.787
that call multiple docs
and spreadsheets together

00:09:50.787 --> 00:09:52.370
to get something
done, or you can have

00:09:52.370 --> 00:09:54.080
just behind one spreadsheet.

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:55.687
You can use Apps
Script to automate

00:09:55.687 --> 00:09:57.770
many of the things that
happen in your spreadsheet

00:09:57.770 --> 00:10:01.010
or to call out to other
services to bring those

00:10:01.010 --> 00:10:02.446
into your spreadsheet as well.

00:10:02.446 --> 00:10:04.070
MELANIE WARRICK: Is
there a public spot

00:10:04.070 --> 00:10:06.734
for people to share Apps
Scripts that they like to write?

00:10:06.734 --> 00:10:08.900
JOANNA SMITH: So not as
much that you like to write.

00:10:08.900 --> 00:10:12.380
We have a pretty active
community across G+,

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:14.649
also across Stack
Overflow and GitHub.

00:10:14.649 --> 00:10:16.940
But there's also a marketplace
where a lot of companies

00:10:16.940 --> 00:10:20.300
will create applications that
they sell to other companies

00:10:20.300 --> 00:10:21.840
to make days better.

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:24.890
And then you can also put
them up for free as well.

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:27.650
Like one of my favorite
isn't actually available.

00:10:27.650 --> 00:10:29.180
To be clear about
this, it was just

00:10:29.180 --> 00:10:32.379
written by someone on our team
and then shared internally,

00:10:32.379 --> 00:10:33.920
but you can make it
yourself as well.

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:37.880
Is, we have a calendar, and
it's a team vacation calendar.

00:10:37.880 --> 00:10:40.340
And the way we manage
it is not by everyone

00:10:40.340 --> 00:10:42.394
putting their vacation
on a second calendar that

00:10:42.394 --> 00:10:44.060
is hard to remember
and you don't really

00:10:44.060 --> 00:10:46.160
want to think about, but
we have an Apps Script that

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:48.701
runs every night at midnight
and looks at everyone's calendar

00:10:48.701 --> 00:10:50.330
and then copies any
event called out

00:10:50.330 --> 00:10:53.000
of office or vacation to
the team vacation calendar

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:54.630
programmatically and easily.

00:10:54.630 --> 00:10:56.300
MARK MANDEL: How do I add
those, are those the add-ons,

00:10:56.300 --> 00:10:56.450
is that a thing?

00:10:56.450 --> 00:10:57.750
JOANNA SMITH: It's
actually different.

00:10:57.750 --> 00:10:59.420
It's not different
but it is different,

00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:00.890
and you're just going to
have to trust me on this

00:11:00.890 --> 00:11:02.010
as I explain it.

00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:05.880
So anybody can add an Apps
Script anywhere pretty much.

00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:08.360
They're great for
mainly the editors,

00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:14.570
so Docs, Sheets, Slides, Forms,
but also in Drive themselves.

00:11:14.570 --> 00:11:18.415
And all Apps Script are ran
from Drive, saved in Drive.

00:11:18.415 --> 00:11:21.770
And the editor you can access
by going to Script.google.com.

00:11:21.770 --> 00:11:26.150
But you can also create a script
inside of a specific sheet

00:11:26.150 --> 00:11:29.210
by clicking on Tools and
Script Editor in there.

00:11:29.210 --> 00:11:31.280
And that script will then
be bound to that sheet.

00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:32.905
So when you share
the sheet, the script

00:11:32.905 --> 00:11:34.490
goes with it to
other people as well.

00:11:34.490 --> 00:11:37.870
That has its own advantages
versus standalone App Scripts.

00:11:37.870 --> 00:11:41.270
And then add-ons are
created with Apps Script.

00:11:41.270 --> 00:11:43.730
They're powered by Apps
Script, but then add-ons

00:11:43.730 --> 00:11:47.430
can span over to
Gmail, to Calendar.

00:11:47.430 --> 00:11:48.690
They're a little more robust.

00:11:48.690 --> 00:11:50.606
And then one of the great
things about add-ons

00:11:50.606 --> 00:11:52.970
is, you write it
all up once and then

00:11:52.970 --> 00:11:56.160
Google does the hard work of
making it work for Gmail on web

00:11:56.160 --> 00:11:57.295
and Gmail on mobile.

00:11:57.295 --> 00:11:58.170
MARK MANDEL: So the--

00:11:58.170 --> 00:12:00.650
you were talking about learning
JavaScript and Apps Script

00:12:00.650 --> 00:12:02.300
and playing with this
stuff a lot more.

00:12:02.300 --> 00:12:03.770
How have you found
the editor experience?

00:12:03.770 --> 00:12:05.870
Because as Joanna touched
on that a little bit,

00:12:05.870 --> 00:12:08.600
how is that integrated IDE
type thing, how does that work?

00:12:08.600 --> 00:12:10.790
ALICIA WILLIAMS: So
for me it's basically

00:12:10.790 --> 00:12:13.260
the difference between even
starting to look at it.

00:12:13.260 --> 00:12:17.210
I didn't want to have to install
any packages on my computer,

00:12:17.210 --> 00:12:19.345
or decide on an IDE.

00:12:19.345 --> 00:12:21.470
I didn't even know what
that stood for at the time.

00:12:21.470 --> 00:12:22.170
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:22.170 --> 00:12:24.860
So having the editor
in the browser

00:12:24.860 --> 00:12:27.770
is really what made it
so accessible to me.

00:12:27.770 --> 00:12:30.440
And you talked a
little bit about how

00:12:30.440 --> 00:12:32.880
to find where people
share these things.

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:35.420
And we do have a lot of
samples in our documentation

00:12:35.420 --> 00:12:36.380
and on GitHub.

00:12:36.380 --> 00:12:39.270
But you'll find that people
share them all over the place.

00:12:39.270 --> 00:12:42.320
And just by using
Search, I tend to find--

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:44.040
I like to start
out with something.

00:12:44.040 --> 00:12:46.010
So if I have nothing
to start out from,

00:12:46.010 --> 00:12:48.290
I'll just take a random
script and put it in there

00:12:48.290 --> 00:12:50.365
and then just
start editing away,

00:12:50.365 --> 00:12:52.490
because I think it's really
helpful to have a place

00:12:52.490 --> 00:12:53.260
to start.

00:12:53.260 --> 00:12:53.550
MELANIE WARRICK: Yes.

00:12:53.550 --> 00:12:54.966
ALICIA WILLIAMS:
So there is a lot

00:12:54.966 --> 00:12:56.540
out there that you can get--

00:12:56.540 --> 00:12:58.800
maybe it's only 5%
of the way there,

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:00.380
but it gives you
a place to start.

00:13:00.380 --> 00:13:02.510
And I find those actually
on Stack Overflow

00:13:02.510 --> 00:13:05.010
as well, there's Apps
Script community there.

00:13:05.010 --> 00:13:07.850
And then we have a lot of
actually Google Developer

00:13:07.850 --> 00:13:11.360
experts that blog about it,
and it's really helped me.

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:15.950
I had to for a demo create a
way to automatically submit

00:13:15.950 --> 00:13:18.680
to a Google form as part of the
demo, which is not something

00:13:18.680 --> 00:13:19.810
you would typically do.

00:13:19.810 --> 00:13:22.070
Typically you would
submit to the form.

00:13:22.070 --> 00:13:24.830
But I wanted to submit
a lot of form responses

00:13:24.830 --> 00:13:26.630
all at once in front
of an audience.

00:13:26.630 --> 00:13:30.710
And I was actually able by using
Search to find somebody that

00:13:30.710 --> 00:13:31.900
had done something similar.

00:13:31.900 --> 00:13:35.180
And it was an area where just
going to our documentation,

00:13:35.180 --> 00:13:37.820
because it wasn't a common use
case, I was getting nowhere.

00:13:37.820 --> 00:13:41.780
So I was pretty excited
and surprised to find it,

00:13:41.780 --> 00:13:44.060
and I was able to then
build that functionality

00:13:44.060 --> 00:13:45.090
into my demo.

00:13:45.090 --> 00:13:46.340
MELANIE WARRICK: That's great.

00:13:46.340 --> 00:13:47.714
You and I are
definitely the same

00:13:47.714 --> 00:13:50.240
in terms of working
with a blank page,

00:13:50.240 --> 00:13:51.950
just grabbing
something, something

00:13:51.950 --> 00:13:55.220
to start out with, I definitely
respect and understand that.

00:13:55.220 --> 00:13:56.897
I know there are APIs.

00:13:56.897 --> 00:13:58.730
Can you tell us a little
bit about the APIs?

00:13:58.730 --> 00:13:59.938
ALICIA WILLIAMS: Oh, the API.

00:13:59.938 --> 00:14:02.420
The APIs are actually
something I haven't really

00:14:02.420 --> 00:14:04.660
delved into yet, but
I think Joanna has.

00:14:04.660 --> 00:14:06.410
JOANNA SMITH: I can
touch on this briefly.

00:14:06.410 --> 00:14:10.370
Basically, we have so many
APIs for so many services.

00:14:10.370 --> 00:14:12.920
And as you would expect,
we've got an API for Sheets.

00:14:12.920 --> 00:14:15.380
So that can basically
provide all the functionality

00:14:15.380 --> 00:14:17.040
that Apps Script
provides as well.

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:19.310
And these are all REST
APIs and we offer them

00:14:19.310 --> 00:14:21.980
through client libraries
in so many languages,

00:14:21.980 --> 00:14:24.500
trying to make sure we can
support any kind of use case

00:14:24.500 --> 00:14:26.420
you have as a developer.

00:14:26.420 --> 00:14:28.090
But the other cool
thing about the APIs

00:14:28.090 --> 00:14:32.210
is, we have a lot that are
specifically really meant

00:14:32.210 --> 00:14:34.910
to be used by a
domain administrator,

00:14:34.910 --> 00:14:38.210
or somebody who's trying
to manage their company

00:14:38.210 --> 00:14:40.862
and uses G Suite, the
enterprise side of it.

00:14:40.862 --> 00:14:42.570
You know, let's say
you're hiring people,

00:14:42.570 --> 00:14:44.800
so you can put them in and out
of your system with the people

00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:45.920
in the Contacts API.

00:14:45.920 --> 00:14:48.110
There's an Admin
SDK that actually

00:14:48.110 --> 00:14:50.970
lets you do all kinds of
controls for your organization,

00:14:50.970 --> 00:14:52.040
but programmatically.

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:54.980
So you don't have to do
everything by going into Chrome

00:14:54.980 --> 00:14:56.960
and clicking, and then
adding, and then typing.

00:14:56.960 --> 00:14:58.751
It's like, wouldn't it
be great if you just

00:14:58.751 --> 00:15:01.100
had a list somewhere and
it all uploaded for you?

00:15:01.100 --> 00:15:02.684
And so, those are
really, really cool.

00:15:02.684 --> 00:15:04.808
MARK MANDEL: That sounds
like a really great chance

00:15:04.808 --> 00:15:06.470
to discuss the
differences between G

00:15:06.470 --> 00:15:09.110
Suite as the free
consumer products

00:15:09.110 --> 00:15:10.232
and G Suite for business.

00:15:10.232 --> 00:15:11.690
Like, what's the
differences there?

00:15:11.690 --> 00:15:14.106
JOANNA SMITH: Well, so at the
core, they're not different.

00:15:14.106 --> 00:15:16.190
Like Gmail, at Google,
Gmail for somebody

00:15:16.190 --> 00:15:19.192
who buys G Suite, and Gmail
for any user out there,

00:15:19.192 --> 00:15:20.150
they're the same Gmail.

00:15:20.150 --> 00:15:22.277
And all the features of
Gmail are there all along.

00:15:22.277 --> 00:15:24.360
But there are some things
that are less available.

00:15:24.360 --> 00:15:27.030
We were talking about
this earlier today anyway.

00:15:27.030 --> 00:15:27.946
ALICIA WILLIAMS: Yeah.

00:15:27.946 --> 00:15:30.660
I mean, we both use
Google Gmail at work,

00:15:30.660 --> 00:15:33.900
and then we use
personal Gmail accounts.

00:15:33.900 --> 00:15:36.510
And yeah, for the most part, I
don't notice a huge difference,

00:15:36.510 --> 00:15:38.100
but I'm also not a domain admin.

00:15:38.100 --> 00:15:40.147
And I think some of the
things Joanna mentioned

00:15:40.147 --> 00:15:42.480
are-- one of the biggest
differences when you're running

00:15:42.480 --> 00:15:44.310
a company, you
need those features

00:15:44.310 --> 00:15:47.310
to be able to manage
email retention,

00:15:47.310 --> 00:15:50.040
or manage drive files
across the company

00:15:50.040 --> 00:15:52.500
that you don't need
to as a consumer.

00:15:52.500 --> 00:15:56.070
There's also some other products
that are for our enterprise

00:15:56.070 --> 00:15:58.080
customers that
we're really trying

00:15:58.080 --> 00:16:00.150
to put out there first
with the enterprise, and--

00:16:00.150 --> 00:16:00.600
JOANNA SMITH: Yeah.

00:16:00.600 --> 00:16:02.580
Like App Maker is are
a really good example.

00:16:02.580 --> 00:16:03.496
ALICIA WILLIAMS: Yeah.

00:16:03.496 --> 00:16:06.060
It's not available to a
consumer user of Gmail,

00:16:06.060 --> 00:16:09.010
but it is available to many
of our enterprise customers.

00:16:09.010 --> 00:16:10.260
MARK MANDEL: What's App Maker?

00:16:10.260 --> 00:16:14.130
JOANNA SMITH: Think if you
had like a UI visual way

00:16:14.130 --> 00:16:16.020
to write an Apps
Script where you could

00:16:16.020 --> 00:16:18.120
drag components
and connect them,

00:16:18.120 --> 00:16:21.250
and the code would be generated
behind the scenes for you.

00:16:21.250 --> 00:16:22.474
And that's what App Maker is.

00:16:22.474 --> 00:16:24.390
So it's a really great
tool for a company that

00:16:24.390 --> 00:16:26.280
doesn't want to spend
money hiring custom

00:16:26.280 --> 00:16:28.049
engineers to build
solutions for them

00:16:28.049 --> 00:16:30.090
and doesn't have a strong
engineering department,

00:16:30.090 --> 00:16:31.950
but has people who
are smart, and clever,

00:16:31.950 --> 00:16:33.220
and can figure it out.

00:16:33.220 --> 00:16:35.640
So that's like a
low code alternative

00:16:35.640 --> 00:16:38.350
to building these kinds
of solutions for yourself.

00:16:38.350 --> 00:16:42.450
ALICIA WILLIAMS: It's also a
great way for people like me,

00:16:42.450 --> 00:16:46.680
maybe, that would have a
sheet and a form as an app,

00:16:46.680 --> 00:16:50.130
and my sheet is my database
to kind of scale beyond that.

00:16:50.130 --> 00:16:53.160
So App Maker, you
can have web forms,

00:16:53.160 --> 00:16:54.880
and you have a database
on the back end,

00:16:54.880 --> 00:16:56.560
but it's a Cloud SQL database.

00:16:56.560 --> 00:17:00.450
So it's kind of a different--
you know, a V2 maybe

00:17:00.450 --> 00:17:04.109
of some sort of app I start to
build just using Google Forms

00:17:04.109 --> 00:17:05.060
and Google Sheets.

00:17:05.060 --> 00:17:06.369
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:17:06.369 --> 00:17:08.550
So we've talked a little
bit about the APIs,

00:17:08.550 --> 00:17:10.530
we've talked about
the Apps Scripts.

00:17:10.530 --> 00:17:12.690
Can you use some of these
extensibility features

00:17:12.690 --> 00:17:15.193
to integrate between
G Suite and GCP?

00:17:15.193 --> 00:17:16.109
ALICIA WILLIAMS: Yeah.

00:17:16.109 --> 00:17:20.460
JOANNA SMITH: So I say yes, and
I also say please, please do.

00:17:20.460 --> 00:17:22.260
Because the way I see,
G Suite is actually

00:17:22.260 --> 00:17:23.770
how people get their job done.

00:17:23.770 --> 00:17:25.740
And if you can make
anyone's job easier,

00:17:25.740 --> 00:17:28.687
then the whole company
becomes a lot more productive.

00:17:28.687 --> 00:17:31.020
And that's like my shtick,
and that's what I'm out there

00:17:31.020 --> 00:17:32.760
and I'm tweeting
and I'm working on.

00:17:32.760 --> 00:17:33.900
MELANIE WARRICK: And
you're sticking to it.

00:17:33.900 --> 00:17:34.860
JOANNA SMITH: Yeah.

00:17:34.860 --> 00:17:36.516
But the other thing
is like, let's say

00:17:36.516 --> 00:17:37.890
several teams
across your company

00:17:37.890 --> 00:17:41.010
all need to work with the data
inside of one of your databases

00:17:41.010 --> 00:17:42.450
through GCP.

00:17:42.450 --> 00:17:43.920
You don't want to
train them on how

00:17:43.920 --> 00:17:45.840
to interact with your
database, especially

00:17:45.840 --> 00:17:47.580
if they don't have
a lot of experience.

00:17:47.580 --> 00:17:50.550
It's too many teams, that's
too much access, right?

00:17:50.550 --> 00:17:52.620
So you can imagine
using like a sheet

00:17:52.620 --> 00:17:54.870
as a front end, where
data can be pulled down

00:17:54.870 --> 00:17:56.670
based upon certain
parameters, or what

00:17:56.670 --> 00:17:59.860
each team needs, and then it can
be re-uploaded from the sheet

00:17:59.860 --> 00:18:00.360
as well.

00:18:00.360 --> 00:18:04.530
But the sheet, the collaborative
and the protective features

00:18:04.530 --> 00:18:06.210
that are built in
by default becomes

00:18:06.210 --> 00:18:08.250
the interface for a
lot of other teams

00:18:08.250 --> 00:18:10.290
to work with data
you're responsible for.

00:18:10.290 --> 00:18:10.920
ALICIA WILLIAMS:
Yeah, and I think--

00:18:10.920 --> 00:18:12.750
I was really excited
at Next this year.

00:18:12.750 --> 00:18:15.760
One of my teammates, Gabe, kind
of sent out an email saying,

00:18:15.760 --> 00:18:17.400
hey, I have this
crazy idea, I want

00:18:17.400 --> 00:18:21.691
to build an end to end IoT app
where we build a device onstage

00:18:21.691 --> 00:18:23.190
and we go through
all of the process

00:18:23.190 --> 00:18:25.380
to actually have
a UI, a front end

00:18:25.380 --> 00:18:27.780
to show people what's
happening with those devices.

00:18:27.780 --> 00:18:31.451
And he wanted to have both an
Angular front end and a Sheets

00:18:31.451 --> 00:18:31.950
front end.

00:18:31.950 --> 00:18:33.660
And I was-- you know,
because I love Sheets--

00:18:33.660 --> 00:18:34.380
I was intrigued.

00:18:34.380 --> 00:18:35.820
I said, oh, Sheets?

00:18:35.820 --> 00:18:38.490
I'm like, the one person on this
team thread that loves Sheets.

00:18:38.490 --> 00:18:39.990
So I started talking
to him about it

00:18:39.990 --> 00:18:42.010
and we ended up
working together,

00:18:42.010 --> 00:18:43.410
and I was part of that talk.

00:18:43.410 --> 00:18:46.380
But what they did was build a
device using a Raspberry Pi,

00:18:46.380 --> 00:18:50.160
so it was taking in heart rate
and temperature information.

00:18:50.160 --> 00:18:53.070
And then they had this
fancy Angular front end

00:18:53.070 --> 00:18:56.020
that was able to show all the
different devices in real time

00:18:56.020 --> 00:18:56.890
what was happening.

00:18:56.890 --> 00:18:59.110
But we also built
the Sheets front end,

00:18:59.110 --> 00:19:01.260
and it was a place that
anybody on the team

00:19:01.260 --> 00:19:04.890
could have pulled time slice
data from our database,

00:19:04.890 --> 00:19:08.250
from our Firetore database, pull
it in, be able to analyze it

00:19:08.250 --> 00:19:11.400
how you're used to a Sheets,
collaborate with others

00:19:11.400 --> 00:19:12.870
on that data as well.

00:19:12.870 --> 00:19:14.550
But then it had a
second function, which

00:19:14.550 --> 00:19:16.380
was to configure the devices.

00:19:16.380 --> 00:19:18.840
So Gabe had said
like, oh you know,

00:19:18.840 --> 00:19:21.420
you can configure the
devices in IoT Core.

00:19:21.420 --> 00:19:23.820
But I'm imagining
if you were going

00:19:23.820 --> 00:19:26.610
to give a bunch of devices
out to people at a company,

00:19:26.610 --> 00:19:28.380
you might have some
staff do that that

00:19:28.380 --> 00:19:30.270
weren't working in IoT Core.

00:19:30.270 --> 00:19:33.330
So this was a way that
those staff could actually

00:19:33.330 --> 00:19:36.030
configure the devices
using Cloud Function.

00:19:36.030 --> 00:19:38.430
So a cell in the
sheet could be updated

00:19:38.430 --> 00:19:41.010
that could trigger a Cloud
Function to be called,

00:19:41.010 --> 00:19:44.087
and then that would update IoT
Core with new configuration.

00:19:44.087 --> 00:19:45.420
MELANIE WARRICK: That's awesome.

00:19:45.420 --> 00:19:46.336
ALICIA WILLIAMS: Yeah.

00:19:46.336 --> 00:19:48.600
And it was actually
really easy to do.

00:19:48.600 --> 00:19:52.910
I think I agreed to do it before
I knew how hard it might be.

00:19:52.910 --> 00:19:53.410
And then--

00:19:53.410 --> 00:19:53.700
MARK MANDEL: It [INAUDIBLE].

00:19:53.700 --> 00:19:54.880
ALICIA WILLIAMS: --I
got really lucky--

00:19:54.880 --> 00:19:55.710
yes.

00:19:55.710 --> 00:19:57.750
I got really lucky
because there's actually

00:19:57.750 --> 00:20:01.620
already a library that I
found that connects Firestore

00:20:01.620 --> 00:20:02.550
to Apps Script.

00:20:02.550 --> 00:20:05.160
So this library--
using a service

00:20:05.160 --> 00:20:07.680
account I could
authenticate to Firestore,

00:20:07.680 --> 00:20:10.890
I could edit my Firestore
database all with Apps Script.

00:20:10.890 --> 00:20:14.040
And so in the end, I had a
few features in my front end

00:20:14.040 --> 00:20:17.190
so I could have-- the user could
put in a certain time slice

00:20:17.190 --> 00:20:20.040
and it would pull that
device measurement data back

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:22.020
and show a graph in my sheet.

00:20:22.020 --> 00:20:26.320
I also had a way to update the
device metadata in Firestore,

00:20:26.320 --> 00:20:28.950
so say like, the
name for the device.

00:20:28.950 --> 00:20:31.110
And then also, the
device configuration.

00:20:31.110 --> 00:20:34.140
So the configuration is
actually stored on the device

00:20:34.140 --> 00:20:35.880
not just in Firestore,
and this needs

00:20:35.880 --> 00:20:37.577
to be done through IoT Core.

00:20:37.577 --> 00:20:39.660
And so this is the part
where I could call a Cloud

00:20:39.660 --> 00:20:42.060
Function just by-- you
know, I can have a trigger

00:20:42.060 --> 00:20:43.690
on my spreadsheet
so anytime someone

00:20:43.690 --> 00:20:46.710
edits the cell I can check
if that cell is edited,

00:20:46.710 --> 00:20:49.530
and then I can call
this HTTP and trigger.

00:20:49.530 --> 00:20:51.930
So it was actually a
pretty cool front end.

00:20:51.930 --> 00:20:54.720
And it exposed a lot of people
in the room who had come just

00:20:54.720 --> 00:20:58.080
to hear about IoT, they got to
hear a little bit about Sheets

00:20:58.080 --> 00:21:00.270
too and a few people
commented on it.

00:21:00.270 --> 00:21:03.900
So I was excited to see that
people saw the value in putting

00:21:03.900 --> 00:21:07.740
Sheets as being a layer
in between technical teams

00:21:07.740 --> 00:21:09.514
and other teams at the company.

00:21:09.514 --> 00:21:11.430
MARK MANDEL: I was
actually going to ask that.

00:21:11.430 --> 00:21:14.130
It sounds like this could
be a really, really probably

00:21:14.130 --> 00:21:17.670
handy or possibly quick way of
getting technical teams to talk

00:21:17.670 --> 00:21:19.655
to the business
users and vise versa,

00:21:19.655 --> 00:21:21.030
and get that
communication going.

00:21:21.030 --> 00:21:23.100
Is that something you're
actively looking to do?

00:21:23.100 --> 00:21:24.290
JOANNA SMITH: Not precisely.

00:21:24.290 --> 00:21:25.800
Like, I'm not here
to restructure

00:21:25.800 --> 00:21:27.850
how Google gets its job done.

00:21:27.850 --> 00:21:30.120
But one thing that's
really important to me

00:21:30.120 --> 00:21:32.350
is making it
easier, like I said,

00:21:32.350 --> 00:21:34.260
this whole idea of
increasing productivity

00:21:34.260 --> 00:21:36.490
with clever and
useful solutions.

00:21:36.490 --> 00:21:39.820
And so if I can explain
something to someone,

00:21:39.820 --> 00:21:41.679
or if I can go to
your company, we

00:21:41.679 --> 00:21:43.470
can give our presentation
for the whole day

00:21:43.470 --> 00:21:47.100
and walk you through it and show
you how simple tools like this

00:21:47.100 --> 00:21:49.800
make it easier for
knowledge to be exchanged.

00:21:49.800 --> 00:21:51.930
That's something that I
think is a huge value, just

00:21:51.930 --> 00:21:54.300
because being able
to communicate

00:21:54.300 --> 00:21:57.070
with other teams who have
a slightly different lingo

00:21:57.070 --> 00:21:59.190
is usually a
barrier, like trying

00:21:59.190 --> 00:22:01.630
to see what you each need
or what you each call it.

00:22:01.630 --> 00:22:04.080
But then if you
have an interface

00:22:04.080 --> 00:22:07.350
like this where it's like, OK,
the engineering side set it up

00:22:07.350 --> 00:22:10.230
and then the sales side
can ask for what they need,

00:22:10.230 --> 00:22:12.786
you actually learn a lot more
about how each person works,

00:22:12.786 --> 00:22:14.160
which can help
you make decisions

00:22:14.160 --> 00:22:18.090
for future growth, or future
databases, or new projects,

00:22:18.090 --> 00:22:18.934
or where to scale.

00:22:18.934 --> 00:22:19.850
ALICIA WILLIAMS: Yeah.

00:22:19.850 --> 00:22:24.660
And the immediate gratification
aspect, teams like IT teams,

00:22:24.660 --> 00:22:26.490
or database admin
teams that are used

00:22:26.490 --> 00:22:28.920
to having to take in
requests and constantly pull

00:22:28.920 --> 00:22:32.880
data for a sales team or
business analysis team,

00:22:32.880 --> 00:22:34.560
you can build this
tool, they can still

00:22:34.560 --> 00:22:37.090
pull the data from a tool
that they are used to.

00:22:37.090 --> 00:22:39.750
So they can ask Sheets
for a time slice of data

00:22:39.750 --> 00:22:41.230
from a Firestore database.

00:22:41.230 --> 00:22:44.520
So it's a way to maybe reduce
some of your own workload

00:22:44.520 --> 00:22:45.592
for repeated requests.

00:22:45.592 --> 00:22:46.800
MELANIE WARRICK: That's cool.

00:22:46.800 --> 00:22:47.310
MARK MANDEL: I
can't remember who

00:22:47.310 --> 00:22:49.330
was talking about it, with like
a BigQuery connector as well--

00:22:49.330 --> 00:22:49.470
JOANNA SMITH: Oh, yeah.

00:22:49.470 --> 00:22:51.030
MARK MANDEL: --so if you want
to be able to get big data out

00:22:51.030 --> 00:22:51.550
as well.

00:22:51.550 --> 00:22:54.606
JOANNA SMITH: So the BigQuery
data connector to Sheets,

00:22:54.606 --> 00:22:55.980
several really
cool things I want

00:22:55.980 --> 00:22:57.390
to make sure I say about this.

00:22:57.390 --> 00:23:00.600
One, Google recognizes that
these kinds of connections

00:23:00.600 --> 00:23:02.820
are popular, and
also, not everyone

00:23:02.820 --> 00:23:04.440
has time to build their own.

00:23:04.440 --> 00:23:06.630
So there's an effort
and a focus internally

00:23:06.630 --> 00:23:08.730
to make sure we provide
some services that

00:23:08.730 --> 00:23:10.380
would be really,
really advantageous

00:23:10.380 --> 00:23:12.507
for large number of customers.

00:23:12.507 --> 00:23:14.340
That being said, the
BigQuery data connector

00:23:14.340 --> 00:23:16.050
is an example of one
that is-- like one

00:23:16.050 --> 00:23:19.500
of the differences between
consumer and enterprise things,

00:23:19.500 --> 00:23:21.960
because it would be part
of your G Suite package.

00:23:21.960 --> 00:23:24.090
But basically
consider it as like,

00:23:24.090 --> 00:23:27.360
we did the work already
for that exact exchange.

00:23:27.360 --> 00:23:29.931
So everything can be done
completely from the UI,

00:23:29.931 --> 00:23:31.680
meaning nobody has to
be trained up on it,

00:23:31.680 --> 00:23:33.990
nobody has to tweak
it, no one in IT

00:23:33.990 --> 00:23:35.430
is stuck being
the one person who

00:23:35.430 --> 00:23:36.900
knows how to maintain the tool.

00:23:36.900 --> 00:23:39.300
From Sheets you can
do a couple of clicks,

00:23:39.300 --> 00:23:42.150
and you can be pulling data
out of your BigQuery database

00:23:42.150 --> 00:23:44.430
even though you don't
really know how to explain

00:23:44.430 --> 00:23:46.050
to anybody what a database is.

00:23:46.050 --> 00:23:48.390
And that's the kind
of ease and simplicity

00:23:48.390 --> 00:23:49.620
that we're going for.

00:23:49.620 --> 00:23:51.557
Because without that,
you spin up your own

00:23:51.557 --> 00:23:53.640
and you have to either
understand the BigQuery API

00:23:53.640 --> 00:23:55.765
or you have to understand
advanced services in Apps

00:23:55.765 --> 00:23:58.780
Script, and then
that's just a lot.

00:23:58.780 --> 00:24:00.030
ALICIA WILLIAMS: And I think--

00:24:00.030 --> 00:24:01.710
I had used this
before because we have

00:24:01.710 --> 00:24:03.960
our data at Google in BigQuery.

00:24:03.960 --> 00:24:06.180
And as a program
manager, I was constantly

00:24:06.180 --> 00:24:09.120
trying to get Google Analytics
data, or that sort of thing,

00:24:09.120 --> 00:24:12.520
to try to figure out
effectiveness of our programs.

00:24:12.520 --> 00:24:15.480
And so I would use the
button in BigQuery,

00:24:15.480 --> 00:24:17.040
that's Export to Google Sheets.

00:24:17.040 --> 00:24:20.476
So you can-- in the BigQuery
UI, you can run a query,

00:24:20.476 --> 00:24:22.350
you see your results,
and you can export that

00:24:22.350 --> 00:24:24.516
to Google Sheets, which I
did all the time because I

00:24:24.516 --> 00:24:26.550
love analyzing data in a sheet.

00:24:26.550 --> 00:24:29.760
But now this basically takes
a step out of the process.

00:24:29.760 --> 00:24:33.060
I no longer have to familiarize
myself with BigQuery UI.

00:24:33.060 --> 00:24:35.130
I don't have to
export it to a sheet,

00:24:35.130 --> 00:24:38.580
I can actually just from my
sheet connect to BigQuery,

00:24:38.580 --> 00:24:40.380
pull my results,
my query results

00:24:40.380 --> 00:24:43.500
are in a tab that has a Refresh
button so anytime I want

00:24:43.500 --> 00:24:45.540
to refresh that data
I can refresh it,

00:24:45.540 --> 00:24:47.760
and then I can connect
it to other data

00:24:47.760 --> 00:24:50.730
in that same spreadsheet or
across other spreadsheets.

00:24:50.730 --> 00:24:53.160
And so it really makes
it possible to access

00:24:53.160 --> 00:24:56.610
BigQuery as somebody
that's a Sheets user.

00:24:56.610 --> 00:24:57.540
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:24:57.540 --> 00:24:59.040
Are there other Cloud
integrations out there

00:24:59.040 --> 00:25:01.800
that you enjoy seeing, or you've
seen that you're excited about?

00:25:01.800 --> 00:25:04.020
ALICIA WILLIAMS: Not
necessarily an integration,

00:25:04.020 --> 00:25:07.920
but an opportunity that I
saw this year that I actually

00:25:07.920 --> 00:25:11.370
talked about, I blogged about,
was using our Machine Learning

00:25:11.370 --> 00:25:12.630
APIs from a Google Sheet.

00:25:12.630 --> 00:25:16.570
So with Apps Script, I
can call any REST API.

00:25:16.570 --> 00:25:19.600
And so seeing that I had
certain types of data

00:25:19.600 --> 00:25:22.690
in my sheet that were hard to
analyze just using formula,

00:25:22.690 --> 00:25:24.880
I was able to use
our Natural Language

00:25:24.880 --> 00:25:27.790
API from a sheet using
Apps Script to analyze

00:25:27.790 --> 00:25:29.080
text data in that sheet.

00:25:29.080 --> 00:25:32.950
So a common use case is people
use Google Forms to collect

00:25:32.950 --> 00:25:33.640
feedback.

00:25:33.640 --> 00:25:35.380
You end up with
free form feedback

00:25:35.380 --> 00:25:38.590
perhaps in a Google Sheet,
which is backing that form.

00:25:38.590 --> 00:25:40.660
And then normally you
would just manually read

00:25:40.660 --> 00:25:43.510
all of those reviews,
get a sense of the data.

00:25:43.510 --> 00:25:46.670
You might get super fancy
and make a word cloud,

00:25:46.670 --> 00:25:48.050
but that's about it.

00:25:48.050 --> 00:25:50.740
So that's not super useful.

00:25:50.740 --> 00:25:53.200
This way I was able
to take that feedback,

00:25:53.200 --> 00:25:55.360
send it over to our
Natural Language API,

00:25:55.360 --> 00:25:57.460
have it extract out
what people were saying,

00:25:57.460 --> 00:26:00.250
what they were talking about
and with what type of sentiment,

00:26:00.250 --> 00:26:03.460
so using our entity sentiment
method of the Natural Language

00:26:03.460 --> 00:26:06.370
API, copy that data back
into my spreadsheet,

00:26:06.370 --> 00:26:09.430
and then I could visualize the
data using-- in Google Charts

00:26:09.430 --> 00:26:11.170
or even out in Data Studio.

00:26:11.170 --> 00:26:13.700
So it's not necessarily
an integration,

00:26:13.700 --> 00:26:14.920
but it's a way to--

00:26:14.920 --> 00:26:17.378
JOANNA SMITH: Wait, how is that
not an integration, Alicia?

00:26:17.378 --> 00:26:18.805
Like, you integrated
with the two

00:26:18.805 --> 00:26:20.230
to build this really cool demo.

00:26:20.230 --> 00:26:22.700
And I also like how you're
like, I'm not excited about it.

00:26:22.700 --> 00:26:24.880
That's one of my
favorite demos this year.

00:26:24.880 --> 00:26:27.430
I talk about that
demo all the time.

00:26:27.430 --> 00:26:28.556
I'm excited about her demo.

00:26:28.556 --> 00:26:30.013
ALICIA WILLIAMS:
I plan to continue

00:26:30.013 --> 00:26:32.050
talking about that demo
for as long as I can.

00:26:32.050 --> 00:26:32.490
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:26:32.490 --> 00:26:33.370
MARK MANDEL: It sounds
like a great demo.

00:26:33.370 --> 00:26:34.250
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah, it does.

00:26:34.250 --> 00:26:34.660
JOANNA SMITH: She's all
like, yeah I know, I just

00:26:34.660 --> 00:26:37.715
did this thing with the
MLAPIs, [INAUDIBLE] Sheets,

00:26:37.715 --> 00:26:38.931
and it kind of counts.

00:26:38.931 --> 00:26:40.930
It's like, no, this is
one of the coolest things

00:26:40.930 --> 00:26:41.680
I can think about.

00:26:41.680 --> 00:26:45.400
And how many people want that
kind of analysis that goes

00:26:45.400 --> 00:26:46.960
beyond a chart, right?

00:26:46.960 --> 00:26:47.740
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:26:47.740 --> 00:26:50.230
I do some internal Google
events with a lot of people,

00:26:50.230 --> 00:26:52.300
and we get a lot of
free form text response.

00:26:52.300 --> 00:26:54.160
And being able to
actually dig through that

00:26:54.160 --> 00:26:55.540
in an automatic
way is fantastic.

00:26:55.540 --> 00:26:57.850
ALICIA WILLIAMS: Well, the code
is available on GitHub, Mark.

00:26:57.850 --> 00:26:58.265
Get started.

00:26:58.265 --> 00:26:59.010
MARK MANDEL: I wish I
could just check it out.

00:26:59.010 --> 00:27:00.400
MELANIE WARRICK: You'll
have to include that link.

00:27:00.400 --> 00:27:02.108
What about other people
that you've seen,

00:27:02.108 --> 00:27:03.419
the work that they've done?

00:27:03.419 --> 00:27:04.960
JOANNA SMITH: It's
not exactly Cloud,

00:27:04.960 --> 00:27:06.880
but you could see how
it could have been.

00:27:06.880 --> 00:27:09.580
One of the things that
amuses me to no end is--

00:27:09.580 --> 00:27:10.990
MELANIE WARRICK: I love how
you both are couching it.

00:27:10.990 --> 00:27:12.475
Well, it's not exactly,
